## Tester prerequisites and evidence boundaries

- Use an explicitly authorized, native Windows x64 machine with native Node.js
  22+. Record the actual OS, processor, Node architecture/version, and tooling.
  A cross-build, PE header check, schema check, simulated test, or another OS
  cannot establish native Windows execution.
- Obtain the descriptor, SHA256SUMS and exact ZIP through the bound URLs.
  Compare the saved descriptor and checksum digests, then the ZIP size and
  SHA-256 **before extraction or execution of any package JavaScript**.
  Preserve the same embedded npm payload/inventory identity throughout testing.
- Plan an isolated test home/channel and preserve existing user content. Do not
  select a payload by PATH precedence or remove an old channel before verifying
  its replacement and retained hook runtime. Destructive purge requires
  separate explicit authorization.
- Do not alter execution policy, quarantine/CFS controls, package exceptions,
  global firewall rules, WinGet policy, or registry policy to make a test pass.
  Report an unavailable prerequisite as NotRun with a separate blocked reason;
  report failed assertions as Failed.
- Keep standalone lifecycle, npm availability, WinGet contract/schema results,
  native WinGet execution, community acceptance, and client discovery separate.
  CI has verified only cross-build/PE/schema/URL/metadata/pre-JS payload integrity.

